    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chang’an was the capital of the Western Han and, after a long break, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang, who built a new city to the south-east of the Han one.</li><li>Its Tang walled city held 800,000 to 1,000,000 people by modern estimates and drew merchants, pilgrims and scholars from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebellion and later sacks destroyed much of it, and in 904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> had it demolished and its people moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Weiyang Palace of the Han, which served as an imperial palace for many later dynasties, covered 4.8 square kilometres, nearly seven times the area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

