    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> moves his court to the Changle Palace</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">191 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The walls of the Han city are completed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">23</span><span class="rf-text">Rebels capture and sack the city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a> is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">582</span><span class="rf-text">Emperor Wen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> sites a new capital, Daxing, south-east of the ruined Han city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">618</span><span class="rf-text">Daxing is renamed Chang’an when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> founds the Tang</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">756</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a>’s forces occupy the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">881</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> occupies the city and makes it the seat of his Qi dynasty</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">904</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> orders the buildings demolished and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The site south of the Wei River has been inhabited since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times, when the Yangshao culture had a major centre at Banpo from about 5000 to 4300 BCE. Fenghao, the twin capitals of the Western Zhou, stood nearby from about 1064 to 771 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> had their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, north of the Wei, from 350 to 207 BCE. Chang’an itself was a small village under the Qin.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> was established in 202 BCE its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a> first ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, as most of his advisors wished. The soldier Lou Jing and the general Zhang Liang persuaded him that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region, the Zhou and Qin heartland along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a>, could support a larger core population and was much better protected, and that Chang’an was far more central in the lands the Han emperor administered directly. His chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-he">Xiao He</a> rehabilitated the Qin Xingle Palace as the Changle Palace, completed in 200 BCE, and began to renovate the Zhangtai Palace as the Weiyang Palace. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, Liu Bang reproached him for the extravagance, and Xiao He answered that the magnificence was needed to overawe rivals and affirm the dynasty’s legitimacy. Thousands of clans of the military aristocracy were relocated to the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%BC%A2%E6%AD%A6%E5%B8%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Chang’an had a population of 146,000 in 195 BCE, when Liu Ying, the Hui Emperor, began work on the city walls. They were finished in September 191 BCE with 146,000 to 290,000 workers serving 30-day corvees and 20,000 convicts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> began a third phase of building that peaked in 100 BCE with many new palaces, and in 120 BCE he turned the Shanglin Park into an imperial park again. By the end of his work two thirds of the area within the walls was taken up by imperial palaces, and nearly three quarters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s nobility lived in Chang’an or near it. It was a consumer city, existing for its role as the political and military centre of China rather than for manufacturing or trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2 CE recorded 246,200 people in 80,000 households in the walled city, and 682,000 in the whole metropolitan region.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang city was a different one. In 582 Emperor Wen of the Sui sited a new capital, Daxing, south-east of the much ruined Han city, and it was renamed Chang’an in 618. Around 750 Chinese records called it a “million-man city”. Most modern estimates put the population inside the Tang walls at around 800,000 to 1,000,000. The census of 742 recorded 1,960,188 people in 362,921 households for Jingzhao, the province that held the capital, and scholars including Charles Benn and Patricia Ebrey agree that the city and its hinterland could have supported about 2,000,000.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Western Han ruled from Chang’an. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>’s short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a> the palaces were altered towards the plans recorded in the Kaogongji. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulin">Lülin</a> rebellion that ended his reign the city was captured and sacked on 4 October 23 CE, and Wang was killed two days later. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and Chang’an was occasionally called the Western Capital. In 190 the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a> took the Han court there, and in 196 it moved back to Luoyang and then to Xuchang.</p><p>Chang’an was the Jin capital from 312 to 316, fell to the Han-Zhao in 316 and was then the capital of the Han-Zhao (318 to 329), the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> (351 to 385) and the Later Qin (384 to 417). Under the Later Qin ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a> it became an important hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Eastern Jin recovered it briefly in 417, the Helian Xia took it in 418 and the Northern Wei in 426. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> (535 to 557) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a> (557 to 581). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> built Daxing in 582, and the Tang, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> in 618, held Chang’an until its downfall. The Weiyang Palace of the Han city served as the imperial palace of many of these dynasties, among them the Xin, the Western Jin, the Former and Later Qin and the Northern Zhou, until its destruction under the early Tang.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chang'an&#8221; cites where it covers Han period, Sui and Tang period:</p><ul><li>Luke Ronald Habberstad, Courtly Institutions, Status, and Politics in Early Imperial China (206 BCE9 CE) (University of California, 2014)</li><li>Nancy Shatzman Steinhardt, Chinese Architecture: A History (Princeton University Press, 2019)</li><li>Charles Benn, China's Golden Age: Everyday Life in the Tang Dynasty (Oxford University Press, 2002)</li><li>Michael Nylan (ed.), Chang'an 26 BCE: An Augustan Age in China (University of Washington Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sogdians.jpg" alt="Sogdians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sogdian_Tribute_Bearers_on_the_Apadana_Staircase_16_(Best_Viewed_Size_%22Large%22)_(4689076272).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A.Davey from Portland, Oregon, EE UU, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Tang city was an irregular rectangle of wards, with eight main avenues dividing it into nine districts; the articles give about 110 walled wards in the outer city, and elsewhere speak of more than a hundred or of 160. A ward held about 50 to 100 families. The city had a Palace City for the emperor, an Imperial City for officials and an Outer City for the people. The Imperial Way, the widest street, was 150 m wide. The two official markets, the East and the West, each covered nine blocks around a central block of government offices. They opened at noon at the sound of 300 drum beats and closed about an hour and three quarters before dusk, and small night markets in the wards went on despite an attempt to close them in 841. The West Market had a Persian bazaar.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> were two of the largest groups. Travellers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Arabian peninsula, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and the east African coast, and some stayed. At least five or six Zoroastrian temples stood in the city, and four around the West Market. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in about 635, and Emperor Xuanzong is said to have decreed a place of worship for Muslims around 742. Emperors also restricted religions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> was banned in 843, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was banned and later restored. Several laws separated foreigners from Han Chinese, and in 779 an edict made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> in the capital wear their ethnic dress. The writer Yuan Zhen called non-Chinese people barbaric and said that their ways would harm the Chinese way of life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A famine in 682, after droughts, floods, locusts and epidemics, hit both Chang’an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. In 843 a fire destroyed 4,000 buildings in the East Market, while the wide avenues kept it from spreading across the city.</p><p>Much of the city was destroyed in the repeated sackings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebellion and the events after it. An Lushan’s and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-siming">Shi Siming</a>’s forces occupied it in 756, and the Tang government and its allies retook it in 757. In 763 the Tibetan Empire briefly occupied it, and in 765 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> and the Uyghur Khaganate besieged it. Rebels occupied it again in 783 and 784.</p><p>In 881 the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a> occupied Chang’an and made it the seat of his Qi dynasty. The Tang regained it in 882, but its forces, though welcomed by the people, looted the city before being driven out, and Huang Chao then carried out a systematic slaughter of the inhabitants. The Tang took it again in 883. In 904 the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> ordered the buildings demolished and the materials moved to Luoyang, which became the capital. The residents and the emperor Zhaozong were forced to move there too. Chang’an never recovered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xian"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xian.jpg" alt="Xi'an" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_wall_of_Xi%27an_51550-Xian_(27959363326).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 904 the Youguo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> was established at Chang’an, and its first governor Han Jian rebuilt it around the old Imperial City. The new town, called Xincheng, was less than a sixteenth of the size of the old city, and the rest was overrun by nature and used for farming. Chang’an was of minor importance until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a>. The Ming gave it the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a> and built walls around the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang palace district, an area about an eighth the size of the medieval city at its height. Some Tang monuments still stand.</p><p>Victor Cunrui Xiong calls the Sui and Tang city the most important city of early imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its grid of walled wards completed a long tradition of planning Chinese capitals and influenced several later East Asian capitals; the Japanese modelled Heijo-kyo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a>) and later Heian-kyo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>) on it, on a more modest scale and without the same fortifications. Archaeologists found two Sogdian tombs in Xi’an in the early 2000s, those of An Jia and Shi Wirkak, which combine Chinese and Sogdian burial customs. The Han Chang’an lay about 5 km north-west of the Ming and Qing Xi’an, though the modern city has grown to include it.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Chang'an&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Victor Cunrui Xiong, Sui-Tang Chang'an: A Study in the Urban History of Late Medieval China (Center for Chinese Studies, University of Michigan, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Chang’an was the capital of the Western Han, and then, in a new walled city to the south-east, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang, when it held 800,000 to 1,000,000 people by modern estimates. Rebellions ruined it, and in 904 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen">Zhu Wen</a> had it demolished. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi’an</a> now stands on the site.</em></p>

